<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
		Opts the app into the device's native screen size. Without a launch
		screen iOS assumes the app predates modern displays and letterboxes
		it: content renders at a legacy aspect ratio with black bars top and
		bottom, not reaching the rounded corners.

		The target sets INFOPLIST_KEY_UILaunchScreen_Generation = YES, but
		that wasn't reaching the built plist, so the key is declared here
		directly. An empty dict is the documented minimum - a plain launch
		screen using the system background colour.
	-->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<!--
		Home screen name. Without these it falls back to PRODUCT_NAME,
		which is $(TARGET_NAME) - so the app showed up as
		"dutyscheduleIOS". Matches the Android app's app_name string.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Duty Schedule</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Duty Schedule</string>
	<!--
		Export compliance. Answers App Store Connect's encryption question
		up front so it stops blocking every TestFlight upload.

		false is the accurate answer here: on iOS the app only uses
		encryption Apple already provides - the Keychain for credentials
		(PlatformSecureStorageApi.ios.kt) and TLS via NSURLSession, which is
		what ktor-client-darwin runs on. It ships no crypto library and
		implements no algorithm of its own. The AES-GCM code in
		PlatformSecureStorageApi.android.kt is androidMain only and is not
		part of this binary.

		Revisit this if the app ever encrypts data itself rather than
		handing it to the OS.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
		The app requests only the notification permission (see Onboarding.kt).
		It never asks for contacts, location or Bluetooth, so these strings
		are never shown to a user.

		They are required anyway: the calf-permissions library covers every
		permission type, so its iOS implementation references CNContactStore,
		CLLocationManager and CBCentralManager. Kotlin/Native links those
		symbols into sharedKit.framework, App Store Connect's static scan
		sees them, and rejects the build with ITMS-90683 unless a purpose
		string is present (contacts is a hard failure, the other two are
		warnings).

		Kept truthful rather than inventing a purpose. If calf-permissions is
		ever dropped or replaced with a notification-only wrapper, these can
		go with it.
	-->
	<key>NSContactsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>DutySchedule does not access your contacts.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>DutySchedule does not access your location.</string>
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>DutySchedule does not use Bluetooth.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
